"The prognosis is influenced by several factors, including a preoperative PSA of 2.5 and an ISUP grade group 2 finding on the prostatectomy specimen. Positive prognostic indicators include negative surgical margins and no documented seminal vesicle invasion or lymphovascular invasion. Conversely, the absence of nodal sampling widens uncertainty regarding regional spread, as no nodes were removed. The biopsy findings showed Gleason 3+4 (ISUP grade group 2) in the right peripheral zone with perineural invasion, which is consistent with the prostatectomy pathology but represents a difference in sampling site and extent.\n\nInformation that would significantly revise this prognosis includes postoperative PSA levels and their subsequent trajectory over time. A rising or persistently elevated PSA following surgery would warrant re-evaluation of the risk profile. The current data lacks nodal status assessment, which limits definitive staging information. The fixed estimated horizon is 84.76 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 55.09–114.42 months; its coverage has not been validated."
